= Solution
{parent=b}

For $a$ prime to $p$, put
$$
b_a(X)=\frac{X^{-a}-X^a}{X^{-1}-X}
=X^{1-a}\frac{1-X^{2a}}{1-X^2},\qquad f_a(T)=b_a(1+T)^{p-1}.
$$
For positive $a$, $b_a(X)=X^{1-a}\sum_{j=0}^{a-1}X^{2j}$ is a <Laurent polynomial>. For negative $a$, use $b_a=-b_{-a}$. Hence it is regular at $X=1$ with value $a$, and $f_a\in\mathbb Z_p[[T]]^\times$ has constant coefficient $a^{p-1}\equiv1\pmod p$. Evaluation at $\zeta_n$ shows that $c_n(a)$ is a <unit> and reduces to one in the <residue field>, so $c_n(a)\in U_n$.

Because $p$ is odd, $\prod_{\xi\in\mu_p}\xi=1$. Both maps $\xi\mapsto\xi^2$ and $\xi\mapsto\xi^{2a}$ permute $\mu_p$, and therefore
$$
\begin{aligned}
\prod_{\xi^p=1}b_a(\xi X)
&=X^{p(1-a)}\frac{\prod_{\xi^p=1}(1-\xi^{2a}X^{2a})}
{\prod_{\xi^p=1}(1-\xi^2X^2)}\\
&=X^{p(1-a)}\frac{1-X^{2ap}}{1-X^{2p}}=b_a(X^p).
\end{aligned}
$$
This rational-function identity also holds for negative $a$ and extends across the removable point $X=1$. It implies $Nf_a=f_a$. Part 1(b) now proves all the required norm compatibilities, and **$c(a)\in U_\infty$**, with Coleman series $f_a$.

Use the additive formal coordinate $z=\log(1+T)$, in which $D=d/dz$. Then
$$
f_a(e^z-1)=\left(\frac{\sinh(az)}{\sinh z}\right)^{p-1}.
$$
Its logarithm after normalization by $a^{p-1}$ is even, so all odd derivatives at zero vanish. For the even derivatives, the <hyperbolic cotangent generating function for Bernoulli numbers> gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{d}{dz}\log f_a(e^z-1)
&=(p-1)\bigl(a\coth(az)-\coth z\bigr)\\
&=(p-1)\sum_{j\geq1}\frac{2^{2j}B_{2j}(a^{2j}-1)}{(2j)!}z^{2j-1}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Here the <Bernoulli numbers> use $B_1=-1/2$. Differentiating $k-1$ more times yields the answer for every index:
$$
\boxed{\delta_k(c(a))=\begin{cases}
0,&k\text{ odd},\\
(p-1)2^k(a^k-1)\dfrac{B_k}{k},&k\geq2\text{ even}.
\end{cases}}
$$
These rational expressions lie in $\mathbb Z_p$ because they equal the integral logarithmic-derivative definition in part (a). In particular $\delta_1=0$; substituting $B_1$ into the even-index formula would give an incorrect answer. For $a=\pm1$ the sequence is one and every derivative vanishes, as the formula also shows.

= 3
{parent=Paper 27}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

The <Iwasawa main conjecture> links two descriptions of cyclotomic arithmetic: Galois <modules> built from abelian extensions, and <p-adic L-functions> built by interpolation of special values. A precise statement must distinguish local <units>, <global units> and the permitted ramification of the extension.

Use global <fields> $F_n=\mathbb Q(\zeta_n)^+$, distinct from the local <fields> $K_n$ in the preceding questions. Put $F_\infty=\bigcup_nF_n$, $G=\operatorname{Gal}(F_\infty/\mathbb Q)\cong\mathbb Z_p^\times/\{\pm1\}$, and $\Omega=\mathbb Z_p[[G]]$. Its pro-p subgroup is $\Gamma\cong\mathbb Z_p$, and choosing a generator identifies its <Iwasawa algebra> with $\Lambda=\mathbb Z_p[[S]]$. The prime-to-p finite quotient has order $(p-1)/2$, so its character idempotents decompose $\Omega$ into one-variable power-series <rings>. Characteristic ideals below are understood componentwise in this decomposition.

Let $\mathcal U$ be the norm limit of local <principal units> in the completions of $F_n$ at $p$, $\mathcal E$ the norm limit of the closures of global <principal units> there, and $\mathcal C$ the closed <module> generated by compatible <cyclotomic units>. Thus $\mathcal C\subseteq\mathcal E\subseteq\mathcal U$. Let $X$ be the <p-ramified Iwasawa module> of $F_\infty$, and let $Y$ be its <unramified Iwasawa module>, equivalently the norm limit of the p-primary <ideal class groups>. In this real tower the relevant character components are finitely generated torsion <modules>; the abelian-field <Leopoldt theorem for abelian number fields> supplies the required rank comparison for $X$.

For a torsion one-variable <Iwasawa module>, the <Iwasawa module structure theorem> gives, up to finite kernel and cokernel, elementary factors $\Lambda/(p^{a_i})$ and $\Lambda/(q_j(S)^{b_j})$ with distinguished irreducible $q_j$. Its <characteristic ideal> is generated by $p^{\sum a_i}\prod_jq_j^{b_j}$; the generator is determined only up to a <unit>. This records all height-one elementary divisors, not just the order of a finite class group.

Let $I=\ker(\Omega\to\mathbb Z_p)$ be the <augmentation ideal>, and let $\zeta_p$ be the even <p-adic zeta pseudomeasure>, normalized by
$$
\int_G x^k\,d\zeta_p=(1-p^{k-1})\zeta(1-k)\qquad(k\geq2\text{ even}).
$$
Even powers are well-defined on $\mathbb Z_p^\times/\{\pm1\}$. The trivial-character pole means that $\zeta_p$ itself need not be integral; $I\zeta_p$ is an integral ideal. In this real, p-ramified convention the main conjecture is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{char}_\Omega X=I\zeta_p.}
$$
On nontrivial even character components this is an equality with the ideal of a <Kubota-Leopoldt p-adic L-function> power series. If $\gamma$ corresponds to $u=1+p$ and $S=\gamma-1$, the corresponding interpolation convention is $g_\chi(u^{1-s}-1)=L_p(\chi,s)$. Other formulations use odd components of the unramified <module> of the full complex tower; <Kummer reflection in Iwasawa theory> introduces the reflected character $\omega\chi^{-1}$ and the accompanying Tate twist/involution. These <modules> and power-series variables cannot be exchanged without those corrections. The main conjecture is a theorem, proved by the <Mazur-Wiles theorem> and also through the cyclotomic Euler-system method.

The <Iwasawa theorem on local cyclotomic units> identifies the purely local quotient
$$
\boxed{\mathcal U/\mathcal C\cong\Omega/(I\zeta_p).}
$$
Its mechanism is visible in Q2. The <Coleman unit-measure exact sequence>, on its even part, has no roots-of-unity kernel or first-moment cokernel, since complex conjugation acts by $-1$ on those terms. It identifies $\mathcal U$ with the measure algebra $\Omega$. The measure comes from the <Frobenius-corrected logarithm>
$$
\mathcal L_f(T)=\frac1p\log\frac{f(T)^p}{f((1+T)^p-1)},
$$
whose kth moment is $(1-p^{k-1})\delta_k(f)$. This follows by applying $D^k$ and the chain rule to the two logarithms. For the <units> in Q2 and even $k$, use $\zeta(1-k)=-B_k/k$ to obtain
$$
(1-p^{k-1})\delta_k(c(a))
=(p-1)2^k(1-a^k)(1-p^{k-1})\zeta(1-k).
$$
Uniqueness of moments therefore identifies their measures with $(p-1)[2](1-[a])\zeta_p$. The factors $(p-1)[2]$ are <units> in $\Omega$, and the differences $1-[a]$ generate $I$. Thus the closed cyclotomic-unit <module> maps exactly to $I\zeta_p$. This local interpolation calculation explains Iwasawa's quotient theorem, including the augmentation correction at the trivial character.

What remains globally is made explicit by <class field theory>. The <class-field unit sequence> gives
$$
0\longrightarrow\mathcal E/\mathcal C\longrightarrow\mathcal U/\mathcal C
\longrightarrow X\longrightarrow Y\longrightarrow0.
$$
Break this into two <short exact sequences>. Multiplicativity of <characteristic ideals> yields
$$
\operatorname{char}(\mathcal E/\mathcal C)\operatorname{char}(X)
=\operatorname{char}(\mathcal U/\mathcal C)\operatorname{char}(Y).
$$
Consequently, using the local theorem, the main conjecture is equivalent to
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{char}_\Omega Y=\operatorname{char}_\Omega(\mathcal E/\mathcal C).}
$$
This is the <characteristic-ideal bridge from local units to the main conjecture>. It shows why the local quotient theorem alone is insufficient: it does not measure how many local cyclotomic-unit classes are supplied by <global units>. The <Euler-system divisibility for real cyclotomic class modules> supplies one containment, while the cyclotomic unit-index and norm-defect comparisons upgrade it to equality. Neither vanishing of class groups nor the Vandiver conjecture is assumed.

There is also the <Iwasawa class-number growth theorem>, often called Iwasawa's theorem in discussions of these towers. For sufficiently large $n$,
$$
v_p(h_{F_n})=\mu p^n+\lambda n+\nu.
$$
The <Iwasawa invariants> $\mu,\lambda$ come from the characteristic series of $Y$; <Ferrero-Washington theorem> gives $\mu=0$ in this abelian cyclotomic setting. This explains eventual linear growth of the p-adic class-number exponent. It is weaker than the main conjecture: the latter determines the complete <characteristic ideal>, including distinguished polynomial factors, rather than only their total degree and p-power content. The local <unit> theorem and the class-number growth theorem are thus important inputs and consequences in the framework, but neither should be mistaken for the global ideal equality itself.

= 4
{parent=Paper 27}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

A <cyclotomic Euler system> must relate <units> at many auxiliary conductors, not just the successive layers of one p-power tower. A useful axiomatization is the <root-of-unity Euler system>. Fix a finite set of excluded primes $S$ containing $2$, and let $W_S$ be the <roots of unity> whose orders are prime to every prime in $S$. A system is a nonzero-valued function $\Phi:W_S\to\overline{\mathbb Q}^{\times}$ with three properties. It is even and Galois equivariant:
$$
\Phi(\xi^{-1})=\Phi(\xi),\qquad \Phi(\xi^\sigma)=\Phi(\xi)^\sigma.
$$
It satisfies the distribution relation at every prime $\ell\notin S$:
$$
\prod_{\rho\in\mu_\ell}\Phi(\rho\xi)=\Phi(\xi^\ell).
$$
Finally, if $\ell\nmid\operatorname{ord}(\xi)$, it satisfies the local congruence
$$
\Phi(\rho\xi)\equiv\Phi(\xi)\pmod{\mathfrak l}
\qquad(\rho\in\mu_\ell,\ \mathfrak l\mid\ell).
$$
The values in this congruence are required to be integral at the prime; in the <unit> example below they are local <units>. Evenness puts the values in the maximal real cyclotomic subfields. Equivariance identifies conjugates, distribution gives exact norm identities, and the congruence supplies information at newly introduced primes. These are the three distinct inputs used in the descent, rather than a single norm-coherence assumption.

Here is a concrete system extending the <units> in Q2. Fix a nonzero integer $a$ prime to $p$ and let $S$ contain $2$ and the prime divisors of $a$, but not $p$. Put
$$
\Phi_a(\xi)=\left(\frac{\xi^{-a}-\xi^a}{\xi^{-1}-\xi}\right)^{p-1}\quad(\xi\ne1),
\qquad\Phi_a(1)=a^{p-1}.
$$
Every value is defined and nonzero: the excluded primes make $a$ invertible modulo the order of $\xi$, and that order is odd. The quotient is the same <Laurent polynomial> $b_a$ used in Q2. Replacing $\xi$ by $\xi^{-1}$ changes both numerator and denominator by a minus sign, so it is even; its rational coefficients give Galois equivariance.

For the distribution axiom, $\ell\notin S$ makes $\ell$ odd and prime to $a$. Both exponents $2$ and $2a$ permute $\mu_\ell$, and $\prod_{\rho\in\mu_\ell}\rho=1$. The calculation in Q2 therefore holds with $p$ replaced by $\ell$:
$$
\prod_{\rho\in\mu_\ell}b_a(\rho X)=b_a(X^\ell).
$$
Raising to $p-1$ proves the axiom. At removable points it holds by evaluating the regular Laurent polynomials, so it includes $\xi=1$ and $\xi^\ell=1$.

For the congruence axiom, a root $\rho$ of order $\ell$ reduces to one at every prime above $\ell$. The integral <Laurent polynomial> $b_a$ thus gives $b_a(\rho\xi)\equiv b_a(\xi)$. These values are local <units> when $\xi$ has order prime to $\ell$: its reduction retains that order, and, unless $\xi=1$, both $1-\xi^2$ and $1-\xi^{2a}$ remain nonzero; at $\xi=1$ the value is the <unit> $a$. The stated power preserves the congruence. Thus **$\Phi_a$ satisfies all three Euler-system axioms**, and its values at $\zeta_n$ are precisely $c_n(a)$. Products, Galois translates, suitable <field norms> and integral group-ring powers produce the other cyclotomic-unit systems needed in the theory.

The distribution relation explains the Euler factors. If $\ell\nmid m$, $\xi$ has order $m$ and $\rho$ is a primitive $\ell$th root, the conjugates over $\mathbb Q(\mu_m)$ run through the nontrivial $\ell$th roots. Removing the single factor with $\rho=1$ from the distribution product gives
$$
N_{\mathbb Q(\mu_{m\ell})/\mathbb Q(\mu_m)}\Phi(\rho\xi)
=\frac{\Phi(\xi^\ell)}{\Phi(\xi)}
=\Phi(\xi)^{\operatorname{Fr}_\ell-1}.
$$
We use arithmetic <Frobenius>, so $\operatorname{Fr}_\ell(\xi)=\xi^\ell$. This is the <cyclotomic Euler norm factor at a new prime>. At a higher power of a prime already dividing the conductor, all $\ell$ translates occur, and the ordinary successive-layer norm relation has no missing-factor quotient. A mere p-power compatible sequence therefore contains only part of the Euler-system information.

To explain the axiomatic method, fix a p-power modulus $P=p^r$ and auxiliary primes $\ell$ with $P\mid\ell-1$, split in the chosen real base <field>. For the cyclic auxiliary <Galois group> of order $\ell-1$, choose a generator $\sigma_\ell$ and define
$$
D_\ell=\sum_{i=1}^{\ell-2}i\sigma_\ell^i,
\qquad N_\ell=\sum_{i=0}^{\ell-2}\sigma_\ell^i.
$$
Subtracting consecutive coefficients gives the concrete identity
$$
\boxed{(\sigma_\ell-1)D_\ell=(\ell-1)-N_\ell\equiv-N_\ell\pmod P.}
$$
This is the <Kolyvagin derivative operator for a cyclic group>. Apply products of these operators to Euler-system values at squarefree auxiliary conductors. The norm relations control the apparent failure of invariance modulo $P$; Kummer descent, using <Hilbert 90> and retaining roots-of-unity corrections where present, turns the derivative data into classes over the base <field>. They have controlled valuations and are unramified away from the selected auxiliary primes and the excluded set. The local congruence relates the valuation component at a newly added prime to the finite residue-symbol component of the preceding class, with the sign and normalization determined by the chosen generator. This finite-to-singular relation is precisely the additional information not obtainable from norm compatibility alone.

The <Chebotarev density theorem> supplies auxiliary prime ideals in specified ideal classes while satisfying the splitting and congruence conditions. Reading the controlled valuations as principal-ideal relations successively bounds the p-primary elementary divisors of the <ideal class group> by the divisibility of the initial cyclotomic-unit class. On suitable nontrivial character components this gives the finite bound $\#A^\chi\leq\#(E/C)^\chi$; passing through the norm tower yields
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{char}(Y)\mid\operatorname{char}(\mathcal E/\mathcal C),}
$$
where divisibility is between characteristic generators, so the associated ideal containment is reversed. The <Euler-system divisibility for real cyclotomic class modules> requires the usual local conditions and control of finite errors; it is not a claim that arbitrary compatible <units> automatically annihilate every class group. The <cyclotomic unit index formula>, together with the global norm-defect comparison, gives the reverse equality. Q3's <exact sequence> then proves the main conjecture.

This account separates the theorem inputs from the explicit calculations: the distribution and congruence identities for <cyclotomic units> are verified above, and the derivative operator is computed directly; the Chebotarev/Kummer descent bounds and the global index comparison are the deeper arithmetic steps. Their usefulness is that the descent uses the axioms rather than a special closed formula for each individual auxiliary <unit>.
